---
// What GDPR Art. 13 requires a site to tell, and Google's Limited Use statement for the calendar.
import { DISPLAY } from '@tonalisk-labs/kit/theme';

import { TEXT_LINK } from '../booking/classes.ts';
import Section from '../components/section.astro';
import Layout from '../layouts/layout.astro';
import { EMAIL } from '../lib/json-ld.ts';
---

<Layout
  title="Privacy policy, marcoferra.com"
  description="What marcoferra.com collects when you book a meeting, why, for how long, and your rights."
  image="privacy"
>
  <article class="flex flex-col gap-6">
    <h1 class={DISPLAY}>Privacy policy</h1>
    <p class="text-slate-700">Last updated 10 October 2026.</p>

    <Section id="who" title="Who is responsible">
      <p>
        Marco Ferra, in Portugal, is the controller of your data. Write to
        <a href={`mailto:${EMAIL}`} class={TEXT_LINK}>{EMAIL}</a>.
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Section id="what" title="What is collected, and why">
      <ul class="flex list-disc flex-col gap-2 ps-5">
        <li>
          <strong>Browsing:</strong> no cookies, analytics or trackers. Your IP address is used to
          deliver the pages and, for up to an hour, to stop abuse of the booking service (legitimate
          interest, GDPR Art. 6(1)(f)).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Booking:</strong> your name, email address, time zone, the time you chose and any
          message, including a Role Conversation's brief. They are used to hold the meeting and send
          its emails (contract, Art. 6(1)(b)). You need to give your name and email address to book.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Paid sessions:</strong> the payment record, kept as Portuguese tax law requires
          (Art. 6(1)(c)). Card details go to Stripe only.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Where you came from:</strong> if your link named where it was shared, that name is
          kept with your booking, to learn which channels work (legitimate interest, Art. 6(1)(f)).
        </li>
      </ul>
    </Section>

    <Section id="processors" title="Who processes it">
      <p>
        GitHub and Amazon Web Services (hosting and the booking service), Google (calendar and
        Meet), Stripe (payments), Maileroo (email) and Proton (replies). Where they process data outside the EEA, they do so under an adequacy decision, such as
        the EU-US Data Privacy Framework, or Standard Contractual Clauses. Calendar data is handled
        under the
        <a href="https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy" class={TEXT_LINK}
          >Google API Services User Data Policy</a
        >, including its Limited Use requirements. Nothing is sold or used for marketing.
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Section id="how-long" title="How long it is kept">
      <p>
        Bookings until you ask for them to be deleted. Payment records for 10 years, as tax law
        requires. IP addresses for an hour at most in the booking service, and for a short time in
        the hosts' logs.
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Section id="rights" title="Your rights">
      <p>
        You may ask to access, correct or delete your data, to restrict or object to its use, and to
        receive it in a portable form, at <a href={`mailto:${EMAIL}`} class={TEXT_LINK}>{EMAIL}</a>.
        You may also complain to the Portuguese supervisory authority, the CNPD.
      </p>
    </Section>
  </article>
</Layout>
